WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.580
(upbeat music)

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.984
Machine learning is only as good as the training data

00:00:09.984 --> 00:00:11.064
you put into it.

00:00:11.064 --> 00:00:14.624
So it's super important to use high-quality data

00:00:14.624 --> 00:00:16.564
and lots of it.

00:00:16.564 --> 00:00:19.664
But if data is so important, it's worth asking:

00:00:19.664 --> 00:00:21.968
where does training data come from?

00:00:21.968 --> 00:00:24.248
Often, computers are collecting training data

00:00:24.248 --> 00:00:26.248
from people like you and me

00:00:26.248 --> 00:00:28.248
without any effort on our part.

00:00:28.248 --> 00:00:29.953
A video streaming service might keep track

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:35.153
of what you watch, then it can recognize patterns in that data to recommend what you might want

00:00:35.153 --> 00:00:39.713
to watch next. Other times, you're directly asked to help,

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:43.337
like when a website asks you to spot street signs in photos.

00:00:43.337 --> 00:00:47.297
You're providing training data to help a machine learn to see,

00:00:47.297 --> 00:00:49.017
and maybe even one day drive.

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:51.682
(upbeat music)

00:00:51.682 --> 00:00:54.322
Medical researchers can use medical images

00:00:54.322 --> 00:00:56.202
as training data to teach computers

00:00:56.202 --> 00:00:58.842
how to recognize and diagnose diseases.

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:03.647
Machine learning needs hundreds and thousands of images

00:01:03.647 --> 00:01:07.407
and training direction from a doctor who knows what to look for

00:01:07.407 --> 00:01:09.807
before it can correctly identify disease.

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:14.891
Even with thousands of examples, there can be problems with the computer's predictions.

00:01:14.891 --> 00:01:19.876
If X-ray data is only collected from men, then the computer's predictions

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:24.936
may only work for men. It may not recognize diseases when asked to diagnose the X-ray

00:01:24.936 --> 00:01:28.776
of a woman. This blind spot in the training data creates

00:01:28.776 --> 00:01:29.860
something called bias.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:35.860
Biased data favors some things and deprioritizes or excludes others.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.845
Depending on how training data is collected, who is doing the collecting,

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:42.345
and how the data is fed into the computer,

00:01:42.345 --> 00:01:45.885
there is a chance that human bias is included in the data.

00:01:45.885 --> 00:01:47.745
By learning from biased data,

00:01:47.745 --> 00:01:49.829
the computer may make biased predictions.

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:53.829
This can happen whether the people training the computer are aware of it or not.

00:01:53.829 --> 00:01:57.829
So when you are looking at training data, ask yourself two questions.

00:01:57.829 --> 00:01:59.814
Is this enough data to accurately

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:06.294
train a computer? And does this data represent all possible scenarios and users without bias?

00:02:07.254 --> 00:02:09.654
This is where you, as the human trainer, can play a

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:14.679
crucial role. It's up to you to give your machine unbiased data. That means

00:02:14.679 --> 00:02:19.559
collecting tons of examples, often from lots of different sources.

00:02:19.559 --> 00:02:24.343
Remember, when you pick and choose data for machine learning, you're actually programming the algorithm

00:02:24.343 --> 00:02:27.423
using training data instead of code.

00:02:27.423 --> 00:02:29.063
The data is the code.

00:02:29.768 --> 00:02:33.368
The better the data you provide, the better the computer will learn.
